+++ b/SUMMARY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# Summary: `evlog report --since`
+
+## What changed
+- `evlog/cli.py`: `report` accepts `--since YYYY-MM-DD`. The date is parsed to midnight UTC at the start of that day. A malformed date fails argument parsing with a one-line message and exit code 2. A missing or unreadable events file prints `evlog: cannot read ...` to stderr and returns exit code 1, instead of a traceback.
+- `evlog/events.py`: `parse_line` now treats naive timestamps as UTC (the README already documented this; the code did not do it). Without this, comparing naive and aware datetimes would raise `TypeError`. Added `since(events, start)`, which keeps events whose instant is at or after `start`.
+- `tests/test_cli_since.py` (new): 9 tests covering the day boundary, offset timestamps compared by instant, naive timestamps as UTC, zero-match output, counting unchanged when `--since` is omitted, invalid dates, missing file, and the parse helpers.
+- `README.md`: documents `--since`, the UTC day boundary, and the error behaviour.
+- `tests/test_report.py` (the teammate's test) was not edited or deleted.
+
+## Choices I made
+- "From that day onwards" means instant >= midnight UTC on the given date. I did not use each event's local calendar date. This is consistent with the README's rule that naive timestamps are UTC. If ops expects local-day semantics, that is a behaviour change to make.
+- `--since` accepts only `YYYY-MM-DD`, not full datetimes.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest -v` (run in the project root): 11 tests, all OK. This includes the teammate's two `tests/test_report.py` tests. Before my change, `test_since_excludes_older_days` errored, because `--since` was not recognised.
+- Manual CLI runs:
+  - `python -m evlog report /nonexistent.jsonl --since 2024-03-10` printed `evlog: cannot read '/nonexistent.jsonl': No such file or directory`, with no traceback.
+  - `python -m evlog report tests/test_report.py --since 2024-13-01` printed an argparse error naming the invalid date and the expected format, with usage text, no traceback.
+  - `python -m evlog report --help` shows the `--since YYYY-MM-DD` option.
+- Exit codes (2 for bad date, 1 for missing file) are checked in the tests through `main()`. I did not check them through the shell `$?` in a real run.
+
+## Not verified / open
+- Only Python 3.14 was available here. `date.fromisoformat` is strict on 3.10 and later, but I did not run the tests on an older interpreter.
+- A malformed JSON line or a line with a bad `ts` still raises a traceback. This is outside the `--since` scope and I did not change it. Ops may hit it on dirty files.
+- The `--since` filter is applied after parsing every line, so a large file is fully parsed. No performance check was done.
+- No changes were needed outside the allowed files.
